d so yeah there so there is kind of a  significant divergence in there whereas  you know where we're at Wood is in a lot  of ways a lot more of a a celebration of  one man's misguided passion whereas this  is one a lot more about one dudes ego  one of the things that I am kind of of  two minds about though is is the the  framing of the framing of the ending the  movie doesn't really hold back like it  it's it's okay with painting Tommy and  in a negative light it it's not a hit  piece it's not out to make him look like  a monster but it's it's also like it's  not afraid to it's also kind of not  afraid to make him look like a monster  when he's being monstrous but at the end  of the film it does kind of fall into  this trap of you know monstrous  directors are ultimately worth its you  know they invoke Hitchcock and Kubrick  you know legendary awful people to work  for and work with and it's like oh but  look at the art that they created and  it's the movie so the disaster artist  ultimately kind of comes down a little  bit more towards you know that like oh  it was all worth it but in in a way it  really kind of is about the perversion  of that out tour theory like that this  very specific manifestation of out - or  theory where it's like you know that by  being  a dictator by being a monster that by  torturing your cast and crew that is how  you create great art and this this is  this is the counter thesis to that where  it's like okay by doing that you created  just accidental arts like the room is  awful it's it's terrible it's not it's  not good  it is legitimately a very bad movie it  just happens to be a deeply  entertainingly bad movie but yeah so  it's it's that you you you have been an  ogre you have been awful to your cast  and crew and you have produced garbage  but that garbage still on some level  speaks to people for none of the reasons  none of the reasons that you intended I  know that Tommy Wiseau has tried to play  off the room as like oh it was always  meant to be a dark that's that's that's  bullcrap just look at it it's clearly it  is clearly 100% sincere and it's just it  is a failure it is a failure of vision  and so yeah so there is kind of this  this perversion of that of that theory  and that it's like you know you can you  can go there you can do all of that and  you can be awful and it it isn't going  to it's not a magic spell for greatness  but at the same time it kind of like the  the final sort of moments of the  disaster artist sort of fall into this  like well if people enjoy it you know if  people enjoy it that's all that  ultimately matters and so yes it was  worth it even if your vision isn't what  ends up coming through and you know  there's so I would I would I would  appreciate that a lot more if the path  there wasn't  you know humiliating his actress and and  stuff like that one of the other things  that the movie doesn't play soft with is  acknowledging that Tommy kind of just  bought his way in like that that the  room exists because tommy was already  wealthy it's it's not some it's not the  product of you know hard work and  gumption it's it's explicitly the anti  product of hard work and gumption it's  it's the product of someone who had no  talent  we got fed up with needing talent hard  work and luck and just bought the thing  that he wanted and it's awful so so that  dimension of it does is is a a vein that  runs through runs through the disaster  artist the fact that Tommy basically  just was able to buy his dream and and  that it's it's it's not the product of  effort it's the product of money and  yeah so so there it's I think it could  have gone a little bit a little bit  harder for that angle of really kind of  embracing the identity of like hey the  room is is ultimately a complete  perversion of our normal narrative of  hardwork luck talent you know but it it  goes a little soft on that but I don't  think that say I don't think that's  that's a fatal flaw it's still a really  funny movie it's still a really  enjoyable movie I had I had an enjoyable  time with it the the two real the two  real moments included  so you know hey yeah you want to see you  guys see it  just see it don't see it
 

